+## 10. Outcome (2026-09-14) — what shipped, what moved, what I got wrong
+
+### Bead A shipped, and my "repo-forbidden" call was wrong
+
+§5 planned cheap reads; I then escalated that the approach was dead because
+`tests/test_no_bare_bd_commands.py` forbids bare `bd` in every tracked file. That
+was too strong, and it was acted on — the mayor dropped Bead A on my say-so.
+
+It then landed correctly in **b743d7f0**, merged 22:40Z. The distinction I
+missed: the guard forbids bare `bd` because bare `bd` resolves its ledger from
+the **current directory** — a pack asset reading whichever store it stands in.
+`bd -C "$RIG_ROOT" --readonly` is not that call. It pins the project to the rig
+checkout the script already reasons about and **fails closed** ("no beads project
+found", exit 1) rather than walking up to `$PWD`.
+
+The implementation extended the policy rather than evading it: a separate
+triple-locked `PINNED_STORE_BD_ALLOWLIST` (marker `bd-pinned-store`) plus a
+regression test proving the exemption is narrow — the same line without the
+marker, the same line pointed at a different directory, and a bare `bd show`
+carrying the marker are all still flagged. Verified at HEAD: 4 passed. It also
+carries a trust check (a pinned answer is used only if it resolves at least one
+of the exact ids asked for, else discard and re-ask through `gc bd`) and reported
+byte-identical results across all three endpoint origins including `explicit`.
+
+The lesson for §5's A4: I framed "does bare `bd` resolve the same store?" as an
+open question for the bead when the repo already had a standing decision. The
+error was not checking for one. But a standing decision is a decision about a
+*mechanism*, and the fix here was a different mechanism — so the correction runs
+both ways, and "there is a guard" was not sufficient grounds to declare the
+direction dead.
+
+### The successor beads
+
+| Was | Now | State |
+|---|---|---|
+| Bead A — cheap reads | **shipped**, b743d7f0 | done |
+| Bead B — cursor advance | **gcp-jkd3** | P1, open, DELIBERATELY PARKED — do not sling |
+| Bead C — home-audit rotation | **gcp-7fgg** | open, unchanged |
+| Direction 2 — move off pre_start | **gcp-d9uj** | P2, open, design pass done |
+
+`gcp-jkd3` is parked pending a comparable candidate set: this rig is fully
+drained (every polecat stopped) with 12 candidate worktrees, and jkd3's own
+description warns the 34% → 81% `scan_complete` jump is confounded by the
+candidate set falling 44 → ~10. Judging a coverage fix there measures the drain.
+
+### §9's pin caveat has expired
+
+§9 said the pin had not moved and to avoid validating against running-agent
+behaviour. The pin **has** moved — `fdfbedd7`, reaper now 1866 lines — and
+gcp-jkd3 reproduced the cursor defect against the new pin at 02:20Z on 09-14.
+
+### The direction-2 constraint was overstated
+
+`mol-witness-patrol.toml:545-551` says the reaper lives in `pre_start` because
+pack assets sit in a content-hashed cache directory "that a formula step cannot
+name." A formula step can, and this repo already does it three times — including
+in that same file at `:808-833` (resolving `polecat-home-audit.sh`), plus
+`mol-deacon-patrol.toml:513-517` and `mol-refinery-patrol.toml:651,676`.
+
+The pattern resolves the pack root from `gc formula list --json` → this formula's
+`.source` → `dirname` twice, with `GC_PACK_DIR` as a fallback that is explicitly
+never the primary (relying on it alone is what made the refinery's approval gate
+permanently unresolvable, gcp-amo). `gcp-d9uj` reuses it verbatim and fixes the
+stale comment in the same pass.
+
+That is the third time in this bead family that a stated blocker turned out to be
+already solved elsewhere in the tree — after `gcp-5u8u` (worktree-keyed scan) and
+§2 here (cursor rotation). Check for shipped machinery before specifying new
+machinery is the standing lesson.
